          %h2#overview Overview
          %p
            F. William Lawvere (February 9, 1937 – January 23, 2023) was an influential
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/category+theory"} category theorist
            and
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/topos+theory"} topos theorist
          %ul
            %li
              %p
                %a{:href => "http://www.acsu.buffalo.edu/~wlawvere/"} institute page
            %li
              %p
                %a{:href => "http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/William_Lawvere"}> Wikipedia entry
                )
          %p
            From an
            %a{:href => "http://at.yorku.ca/t/o/p/c/06.htm"} interview
            with
            = succeed ":" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/John+Isbell"} John Isbell
          %blockquote
            %p “Talking with Bill, I often feel like a fly buzzing around a cow. (It seems to me I can liken Bill to a cow, if I’m just a fly myself.) On any easy question, I’ll probably see the answer first. But his thoughts seem to move on a level where I don’t function, I can barely see down there.”
          %p See
          %ul
            %li#Interview07
              %em> An interview with F. William Lawvere
              ,
              %a{:href => "http://www.cim.pt/?q=publications"} Bulletin of the International Center for Mathematics
              (
              %a{:href => "http://www.cim.pt/files/publications/b23www.pdf"}> part 1, December 2007
              ,
              = succeed "," do
                %a{:href => "http://www.cim.pt/files/publications/b24www.pdf"} part 2, June 2008
              = succeed "," do
                %a{:href => "http://www.mat.uc.pt/~picado/lawvere/interview.pdf"} full pdf
              = succeed ")" do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/files/LawvereInterview.pdf", :title => "pdf"} pdf
          %p for a survey of his academic path and work. See also
          %ul
            %li
              Interview by Felice Cardone, March 2013 (
              %a{:href => "https://dl.dropboxusercontent.com/u/92056191/Archive/temporary_new_material/lawvere-cardone-2000.html"}> link
              )
          %p
            for an interview on his contributions to
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/categorical+logic"} categorical logic
          %p
            A recording of the commemorative special session in honour of Bill Lawvere from CT2023 can be found here: (
            %a{:href => "https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=x23KS3VOpRg&list=PLu4STGsfbix9l6rPxGsjG6Gl822k1hj2X&index=1&pp=iAQB"}> video
            )
          %p
            For a (somewhat random) list of further links see also at “
            %a{:href => "http://conceptualmathematics.wordpress.com/2013/02/09/happy-birthday-professor-f-william-lawvere/"}> conceptual mathematics
            ”.
          %h2#topics Topics
          %h3#main_contributions Main contributions
          %p
            Lawvere invented
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/categorical+logic"} categorical logic
            and introduced the eponymous
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Lawvere+theories"} Lawvere theories
            as a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/category+theory"} category-theoretic
            way to describe finitary
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/algebraic+theories"} algebraic theories
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Grothendieck+toposes"} Grothendieck toposes
            to
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/elementary+toposes"} elementary toposes
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/foundations"} foundations
            of
            = succeed ";" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/mathematics"} mathematics
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/foundation"} foundation
            in
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/structural+set+theory"} structural set theory
            called
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/ETCS"} ETCS
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/synthetic+differential+geometry"} synthetic differential geometry
            as a foundation for
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/differential+geometry"} differential geometry
            and
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/equations+of+motion"} equations of motion
            in
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/continuum+physics"} continuum physics
            %em
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/cohesive+topos"} cohesive topos
            as a more general foundation of
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/geometry"} geometry
          %h3#MotivationFromFoundationsOfPhysics Mathematics relating to Physics
          %p
            A central motivation for Lawvere’s work is the search for a good mathematical
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/foundations"} foundations
            of
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/physics"} physics
            = succeed ")" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/classical+physics"} classical
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/continuum+mechanics"} continuum mechanics
            (or at least some
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/kinematics"} kinematical
            aspects thereof, Lawvere does not seem to mention
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Hamiltonians"} Hamiltonians
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Lagrangians"} Lagrangians
            or
            = succeed ")." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/action+functionals"} action functionals
          %p
            In (
            %a{:href => "http://www.mat.uc.pt/~picado/lawvere/interview.pdf"}> interview, p. 8
            ) he recalls:
          %blockquote
            %p
              I had been a student at Indiana University from 1955 to January 1960. I liked
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/experiment"} experimental
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/physics"} physics
              but did not appreciate the imprecise reasoning in some theoretical courses. So I decided to study
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/mathematics"} mathematics
              first.
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Clifford+Truesdell"} Truesdell
              was at the Mathematics Department but he had a great knowledge in Engineering Physics. He took charge of my education there.
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                    %mo .
                    %mo .
                    %mo .
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} [...]
              in 1955 (and subsequently) had advised me on pursuing the study of
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/continuum+mechanics"} continuum mechanics
              and
              = succeed "." do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/kinetic+theory"} kinetic theory
            %p
              In Summer 1958 I studied
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/topological+dynamics"} Topological Dynamics
              with George Whaples, with the agenda of understanding as much as possible in categorical terms.
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                    %mo .
                    %mo .
                    %mo .
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} [...]
              Categories would clearly be important for simplifying the foundations of
              = succeed "." do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/continuum+physics"} continuum physics
          %p
            Then in (
            %a{:href => "http://www.mat.uc.pt/~picado/lawvere/interview.pdf"}> interview, p. 11
            ) about the early 1960s:
          %blockquote
            %p
              I felt a strong need to learn more
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/set+theory"} set theory
              and
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/logic"} logic
              from experts in that field, still of course with the aim of clarifying the foundations of
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/category+theory"} category theory
              and of
              = succeed "." do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/physics"} physics
          %p
            The title of the early text
            = succeed "," do
              %em
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Toposes+of+laws+of+motion"} Toposes of laws of motion
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/synthetic+differential+geometry"} synthetic differential geometry
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/classical+mechanics"} classical mechanics
          %p
            On this, in (
            %a{:href => "http://www.mat.uc.pt/~picado/lawvere/interview.pdf"}> interview, p. 15
            ):
          %blockquote
            %p
              Q: As an assistant professor in Chicago, in 1967, you taught with Mac Lane a course on
              = succeed "," do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/mechanics"} Mechanics
              = succeed "\u201D." do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/synthetic+differential+geometry"} synthetic differential geometry
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Categorical+dynamics"} Categorical Dynamics
              and
              = succeed "?" do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/synthetic+differential+geometry"} Synthetic Differential Geometry
            %p
              A: From January 1967 to August 1967 I was Assistant Professor at the University of Chicago. Mac Lane and I soon organized to teach a joint course based on Mackey’s book “Mathematical Foundations of
              = succeed "\u201D." do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/quantum+mechanics"} Quantum Mechanics
            %p Q: So, Mackey, a functional analyst from Harvard mainly concerned with the relationship between quantum mechanics and representation theory, had some relation to category theory.
          %p
            Then (
            %a{:href => "http://www.mat.uc.pt/~picado/lawvere/interview.pdf"}> interview, p. 16
            ):
          %blockquote
            %p
              Q: Back to the origins of Synthetic Differential Geometry, where did the idea of organizing such a joint course on Mechanics originate ? Apparently, Chandra had suggested that Saunders give some courses relevant to physics, and our joint course was the first of a sequence. Eventually Mac Lane gave a talk about the
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Hamilton-Jacobi+equation"} Hamilton-Jacobi equation
              at the Naval Academy in summer 1970 that was published in the American Mathematical Monthly
            %p
              A:
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                    %mo .
                    %mo .
                    %mo .
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} [...]
              I began to apply the Grothendieck topos theory that I had learned from Gabriel to the problem of simplied foundations of
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/continuum+mechanics"} continuum mechanics
              as it had been inspired by
              = succeed "\u2018s" do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Clifford+Truesdell"} Truesdell
              = succeed "\u2019s" do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Walter+Noll"} Noll
              = succeed "," do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/axiom"} axiomatizations
              = succeed "." do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/topological+dynamics"} topological dynamics
          %p
            A review of this with more comments on more relations to physics is in the introduction to the book collection
            = succeed "," do
              %em
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Categories+in+Continuum+Physics"} Categories in Continuum Physics
          %p
            In the talk
            %em
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Toposes+of+laws+of+motion"} Toposes of laws of motion
            in 1997, Lawvere starts with the following remark
          %blockquote
            %p
              I read somewhere recently that the basic program of infinitesimal
              = succeed "," do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/calculus"} calculus
              = succeed "," do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/continuum+mechanics"} continuum mechanics
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/differential+geometry"} differential geometry
              is that all the world can be reconstructed from the
              = succeed "." do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/infinitesimal+object"} infinitely small
            %p
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                    %mo .
                    %mo .
                    %mo .
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} [...]
              I think that on the basis of these developments we can focus on this question of making very explicit how
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/continuum+physics"} continuum physics
              etc. can be built up mathematically from very simple ingredients.
          %p
            In the same talk, a few lines later after discussion of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/infinitesimally+thickened+points"} infinitesimally thickened points
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi T
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} T
          %blockquote
            %p
              The basic
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/spaces"} spaces
              which are needed for
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/functional+analysis"} functional analysis
              and theories of
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/physical+fields"} physical fields
              are thus in some sense available in any
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/topos"} topos
              with a suitable
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/object"} object
              = succeed "." do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi T
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} T
          %p
            In 2000 in the article
            %em
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Comments+on+the+development+of+topos+theory"} Comments on the development of topos theory
            Lawvere writes in the closing section 7 titled “From and to continuum physics”:
          %blockquote
            %p
              What was the impetus which demanded the simplification and generalization of
              = succeed "\u2018s" do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Grothendieck"} Grothendieck
              = succeed "," do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/topos"} topos
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/logic"} logic
              and
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/set+theory"} set theory
              were not decisive.
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                    %mo .
                    %mo .
                    %mo .
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} [...]
              My own motivation came from my earlier study of
              = succeed "." do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/physics"} physics
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/foundation"} foundation
              of the
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/continuum+physics"} continuum physics
              of general materials, in the spirit of
              = succeed "," do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Clifford+Truesdell"} Truesdell
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Walter+Noll"} Noll
              and others, involves powerful and clear physical ideas, which unfortunately have been submerged under a mathematical apparatus
              = succeed "." do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                      %mo .
                      %mo .
                      %mo .
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} [...]
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                    %mn 25
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} [25]
              lamented, all this apparatus gives often a very uncertain fit to the phenomena. The apparatus may well be helpful in the solution of certain problems, but can the problems themselves and the needed axioms be stated in a direct and clear manner? And might this not lead to a simpler, equally rigorous account? These were the questions to which I began to apply the
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/topos"} topos
              method in my 1967 Chicago lectures &amp;lbrack;
              %em>
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Categorical+dynamics"} Categorical dynamics
              \&amp;rbrack;. It was clear that work on the notion of topos itself would be needed to achieve the goal. I had spent 1961-62 with the Berkeley logicians, believing that listening to experts on
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/foundations"} foundations
              might be the road to clarifying foundational questions.
            %p
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                    %mo .
                    %mo .
                    %mo .
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} [...]
              Several books treating the simplified
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/topos+theory"} topos theory
              (
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Sheaves+in+Geometry+and+Logic"} MacLane-Moerdijk
              being the most recent and readable text), together with the three excellent books on
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/synthetic+differential+geometry"} synthetic differential geometry
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                    %mo .
                    %mo .
                    %mo .
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} [...]
              provide a solid basis on which further treatment of
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/functional+analysis"} functional analysis
              and the needed development of
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/continuum+physics"} continuum physics
              can be based.
          %p
            The
            %a{:href => "http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/William_Lawvere"} Wikipedia entry
            concludes:
          %blockquote
            %p Lawvere continues to work on his 50-year quest for a rigorous flexible base for physical ideas, free of unnecessary analytic complications.
          %p
            See also at
            %em
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/higher+category+theory+and+physics"} higher category theory and physics
            for more on this.
          %h3#RelationToPhilosophy Mathematics relating to Philosophy
          %p
            Lawvere has proposed formalizations in
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/category+theory"} category theory
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/categorical+logic"} categorical logic
            and
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/topos+theory"} topos theory
            of concepts which are motivated from
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/philosophy"} philosophy
            = succeed "\u2018s" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Georg+Hegel"} Georg Hegel
            %em
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Science+of+Logic"} Science of Logic
            (see there for more). This includes for instance definitions of concepts found there such as:
          %ul
            %li
              %em>
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/objective+and+subjective+logic"} objective and subjective logic
              ,
              = succeed "," do
                %em
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/abstract+general"} abstract general
              = succeed "," do
                %em
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/concrete+general"} concrete general
              = succeed "," do
                %em
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/concrete+particular"} concrete particular
              = succeed "," do
                %em
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/unity+of+opposites"} unity of opposites
              = succeed "," do
                %em
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Aufhebung"} Aufhebung
              = succeed "," do
                %em
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/being"} being
              = succeed "," do
                %em
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/becoming"} becoming
              = succeed "," do
                %em
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/space+and+quantity"} space and quantity
              = succeed "," do
                %em
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/cohesion"} cohesion
              %em
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/intensive+and+extensive+quantity"} intensive and extensive quantity
          %p (see the references in these entries for pointers).
          %p
            In (
            %a{:href => "#Lawvere92"}> Lawvere 92
            ) it says:
          %blockquote
            %p It is my belief that in the next decade and in the next century the technical advances forged by category theorists will be of value to dialectical philosophy, lending precise form with disputable mathematical models to ancient philosophical distinctions such as general vs. particular, objective vs. subjective, being vs. becoming, space vs. quantity, equality vs. difference, quantitative vs. qualitative etc. In turn the explicit attention by mathematicians to such philosophical questions is necessary to achieve the goal of making mathematics (and hence other sciences) more widely learnable and useable. Of course this will require that philosophers learn mathematics and that mathematicians learn philosophy.
          %p
            A precursor to this undertaking is
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Hermann+Grassmann"} Hermann Grassmann
            with his
            %em
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Ausdehnungslehre"} Ausdehnungslehre
            (
            %a{:href => "#Lawvere95"}> Lawvere 95
            ), see there for more.
          %h2#Talks Talks, Lecture notes and Publications
          %p
            The following is a list of texts by Lawvere, equipped with brief comments and hyperlinks to further material on the
            = succeed "Lab." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi n
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} n
          %ul
            %li
              %p
                %a{:href => "https://lawverearchives.com/"} The Lawvere Archives
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Matt+Earnshaw"}> Matt Earnshaw
                \:
                %a{:href => "https://github.com/mattearnshaw/lawvere"} repository of Lawvere’s publications
          %p
            (Some of Lawvere’s writings don’t exist as published articles, but circulate in some form or other. Notably the “
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Archive+for+Mathematical+Sciences+%26+Philosophy"}> Archive for Mathematical Sciences Philosophy
            ” run by
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Michael+Wright"} Michael Wright
            has a lot of recordings or lectures by Lawvere, though presently few or none of the files in the archive are available online.)
          %h3#general General
          %ul
            %li#Lawvere62
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/William+Lawvere"}> William Lawvere
                \:
                = succeed "," do
                  %em The category of probabilistic mappings
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Gian-Carlo+Rota"} Gian-Carlo Rota
                (1962) &amp;lbrack;
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/files/Lawvere-ProbMappings.pdf", :title => "pdf"}> pdf
                \&amp;rbrack;
              %p posthumously retyped as:
              %p#Lawvere62b
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Tobias+Fritz"} Tobias Fritz
                (ed.):
                = succeed "," do
                  %em The Category of Probabilistic Mappings With Applications to Stochastic Processes, Statistics, and Pattern Recognition
                %a{:href => "https://lawverearchives.com"} Lawvere Archive
                (2025) &amp;lbrack;
                %a{:href => "https://lawverearchives.com/wp-content/uploads/2025/07/1962.probmap.pdf"}> pdf
                \&amp;rbrack;
              %blockquote
                %p
                  (on
                  = succeed ")" do
                    %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/category-theoretic+approaches+to+probability+theory"} category-theoretic approaches to probability theory
            %li
              %p
                %em
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Functorial+Semantics+of+Algebraic+Theories"} Functorial Semantics of Algebraic Theories
                Originally published as: Ph.D. thesis, Columbia University (1963) and in Reports of the Midwest Category Seminar II, 1968, 41-61, Republished in: Reprints in Theory and Applications of Categories, No. 5 (2004) pp 1-121 (
                %a{:href => "http://www.tac.mta.ca/tac/reprints/articles/5/tr5abs.html"}> tac
                )
              %blockquote
                %p
                  (on
                  = succeed "," do
                    %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/algebraic+theories"} algebraic theories
                  = succeed ")" do
                    %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Lawvere+theories"} Lawvere theories
            %li
              %p
